    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get straight to the point with the technical specifics for your cedar deck in Vancouver. The local building codes will dictate the live load requirements, which typically include a minimum of 40 pounds per square foot (psf) for residential decks. This live load accounts for the weight of people, furniture, and any other temporary loads that might be on the deck.\n\nFor the dead load, which includes the weight of the deck structure itself, you'll need to calculate the weight of the cedar planks, joists, fasteners, and any other permanent fixtures. The dead load is usually less than the live load, but it's still an important factor in determining the overall load on your beams and posts.\n\nTo ensure your deck is safe and up to code, you'll want to add the dead load and the live load together to get the total load. Then, you'll need to select beams and posts that can support this total load, taking into account the span of the deck and any additional factors such as wind or snow loads, which can also affect the structural requirements.\n\nRemember to check the latest Vancouver building codes or consult with a structural engineer to get the most accurate and up-to-date information for your specific project.",
